Graded:   tester
Case:     AWPTCM-T22655 (Draft, folder /Modbus/Proj 2166 Modbus Support), objective "dynamic
          change of server configuration and confirm it is reflected correctly", precondition
          "modbus is configured and client able to retrieve information", 4 steps
Build:    stk_a main-calanm (IE520-tb470.rel, build date Tue Oct 6 19:44:28 UTC 2026),
          bootloader 9.2.0, on members 1, 3 and 4
Configs:  stk_a.cfg (running-config after setup; saved to startup: no)

VERDICT: PASS -- TCP port change, server disable/enable, API port disable/enable and CLI-to-register reflection all confirmed
      dynamic TCP port change 502 -> 5020 .... PASS  (:502 refused, :5020 answers READ 0x0000 = 5;
                                                      DUT logs "TCP Port changed to 5020"; reversed
                                                      the same way)
      dynamic server disable / enable ........ PASS  (disabled: :502 refused; enabled: :502 answers
                                                      READ 0x0000 = 5, Write access still enabled)
      API disables / enables port1.0.2 ....... PASS  (write 0x500e = 0x0000 -> CLI "administrative
                                                      state is DOWN", "user shutdown", 0x500d/0x500e
                                                      = 0000; write 0xf000 -> UP, f103/f000; each
                                                      write answered exception 4, see CAVEAT)
      CLI shutdown reflected over Modbus ..... PASS  (CLI shutdown -> 0x500d/0x500e = 0000; no
                                                      shutdown -> f103/f000)
CAVEAT:  every linked stack port reachable on this bench is a static-aggregator member (port3.0.13
         is the client's own link and cannot be shut), so STEP 3 was exercised on port1.0.2, a
         member of sa2. There the DUT applies the port state change but answers each write with an
         exception (function code 134 = 0x86; the server's "slave device failure" counter, code 4,
         goes 5 -> 7 over the two writes) and logs "Failed to set Auto duplex / polarity / speed for
         port1.0.2." plus "MODBUS: Request failed". The same write on port1.0.1 (not an aggregator
         member) is acknowledged "ok (echo ...)", but port1.0.1 has no transceiver ("not present"),
         so there only its administrative state moves; no link was taken down on a non-member port.

